#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519608} :activity/id #uuid "1ab91b77-5a38-545c-aad3-8c7b75ae2ef4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113 :topic/id #uuid "dba97a8b-19ad-5a52-bd24-261e0b206661" :topic/math-academy-id 1968} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b5beb30-0a75-5fc1-9136-9135683b1b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346129} {:answer/id #uuid "70a5b71d-efb0-58ab-af42-12eed66f2bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346339} {:answer/id #uuid "783aadac-abc2-5341-8883-bae757b2fee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -11\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346406} {:answer/id #uuid "78aeeb6e-6d38-58dc-93e3-4da1e56572c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346408} {:answer/id #uuid "df14a525-a2b3-5a1d-8fb5-695c415e41e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347312} :answer-field/id #uuid "feb4a3c4-e3c2-59e1-b4f8-a58de2ef0e9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e9500f77-53af-537c-9030-31932253fda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80372" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "003b1825-afe9-583d-9089-86405c5e6a74" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e63821f-3c2e-5f90-999d-3556766249b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345617} {:answer/id #uuid "41d1a660-210a-538d-a83e-3694472cfec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345914} {:answer/id #uuid "941da20b-d6b9-5332-879f-51c1830cdd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346644} {:answer/id #uuid "e63b7ab8-5e9a-5b89-94b9-a182e32d7d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347381} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8851cf-bb44-563a-8b2f-b5f9da98356d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186347381} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c777445-6342-54ff-86ad-194f947964f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "93fc1e8e-53c7-5d4c-ad22-c729f51f630d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80464" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "1b44f557-01ab-5482-9558-b2e2cf4a9a80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ff14a7-d4d5-50bc-821b-a293afe61e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345647} {:answer/id #uuid "65179ae5-e8f1-5311-9f64-d3365ae764c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346231} {:answer/id #uuid "bca55efc-20ac-5249-a6ef-973336e68518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346991} {:answer/id #uuid "c5248413-a636-5fb2-a3f0-58309d0188c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347058} {:answer/id #uuid "e83ddb93-7529-5d0f-bd36-1ccfb06d3e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "D=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345647} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d0c9e7-8b7e-5689-a456-e913f44eca5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48f981b1-9aeb-5e6b-818c-36bd836ee49e" :question/math-academy-id "q-80455" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & -12 \\\\ 4 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ed0c797-27ac-5a1d-9d59-6db1aaefb34a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b9fc411-d727-58c3-b130-7cb8806396aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455067} {:answer/id #uuid "6651a84a-abb1-53a5-8762-4f56861de479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455088} {:answer/id #uuid "85b5f122-b544-5270-a1c3-306a799a62e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455143} {:answer/id #uuid "b787992e-f51f-5213-a591-68b1b37d8b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455225} {:answer/id #uuid "c81b241b-7949-5f59-a9cc-99f9b9e79c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455246}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455067} :answer-field/id #uuid "86414dc6-5142-5c5b-92b4-cd993eb839dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dde0c5ba-d69f-5523-84e3-29d85ec2262a" :question/math-academy-id "q-80391" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $E=\\begin{bmatrix}-32 & 40 \\\\ -30 & 38\\end{bmatrix}.$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8$ are the eigenvalues of $E$, and\n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$, ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=8.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3bdb7801-f8d6-5f41-a4ac-0d153c4f6c8b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519618}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5f320c-678c-588f-97ca-59e7f48ab67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270224} {:answer/id #uuid "e976cf72-85c5-52fb-8497-4a6b96dccdff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271311} {:answer/id #uuid "e9961cc7-e783-51ec-bc2d-47d357514968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271315} {:answer/id #uuid "f4309c69-9db1-5dc4-aa51-0b57a127e2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271410} {:answer/id #uuid "fb64b1dd-5faf-5d58-a38e-7a3c3bf0ddab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a6aca89-aa2e-56f0-bbd1-0f5adf3139d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "472ad991-cd49-54f3-aa78-cfe11b836a0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140053" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 \\\\ -3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{3}{1}=3.$"} :step/id #uuid "f7118053-34d6-53bc-9f06-3bdde789e2a3"}] :activity/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518977}]}
